# docs/AGENTS.md

Operational schema for the `docs/` tree in **{{repo_name}}**. This file, with the task references it routes to (§2.A), is the single source of truth for everything an agent does under `docs/`. `init-docs` creates a repo-root `AGENTS.md` when the repository root holds no `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` in any letter case. A `CLAUDE.local.md` does not block that create. When an exact `AGENTS.md` exists, `init-docs` only appends to it and rewrites nothing. The created file and the appends carry the same two items: the line "See `<docs_dir>/AGENTS.md` for documentation operations." unless the file already references this one, and an instruction to read `<docs_dir>/objectives.md` unless the file already names `objectives.md`. `init-docs` writes nothing to a `CLAUDE.md` in any letter case, to a case variant of `AGENTS.md`, or through a symlink. It reports each such entry with the remedy that applies to it. Here, as everywhere in this file, `docs/` and `<docs_dir>` both name the configured tree directory, `bionic` by default. The `See` line is plain text, not an include.

## 1. What this `docs/` directory is

A project-scoped documentation tree maintained by the `crux` Claude Code plugin. It covers seven concerns: **code docs** (regenerated from source), **research wiki** (curated external knowledge), **ADRs** (architecture decisions), **briefs** (pre-decision exploration), **work journal** (narrative work record), **promptbooks** (plan-of-prompts artifacts with run snapshots), and **invariants** (pinned, ratified, executable statements of what must be true — a ledger plus an executable check suite, both under `<docs_dir>/invariants/` (the suite in `checks/`), per §15) — plus a default-on, derived **arch** spine (the project's own architecture, regenerated on demand by `derive-arch`; the primary current-state surface, per §4) and the **observations** concern (`governs`-shaped records of what the code already does, evidenced by `path:line-range` rather than decided; additive, default-on for new repos, opt-in for existing trees, per §17). The invariants concern is enabled at `schema_version: "5"`. The plugin and its skills are installed via Claude Code's plugin marketplace flow, or via Codex's own plugin marketplace flow. Skill prose that must stay correct across both runtimes uses crux's portable `CRUX_PLUGIN_ROOT` bridge name instead of assuming any one runtime's loader-set variable is universal (see §7.A). This file is the *operational* contract; the plugin's `README.md` and `templates/` are the *distribution* contract.

## 2. Ownership

The optional standalone `micro-crux.md` guide explains this knowledge tree,
supported record formats, bounded changes, and manual maintenance. Say
"install micro-crux" to install it with an explicit root `AGENTS.md` read
instruction. Initialization does not install it automatically. The guide
preserves this project's policy: an unavailable required writer or approval
remains a specific unmet prerequisite, captured in the existing inbox.

Claude owns every write under `docs/` **except**:

- `docs/briefs/*` — draft material that ADRs consume; **pre-decision exploration**, not a decision. Two authorship paths: (a) **human-authored** — Claude may scaffold a brief with `BRIEF-template.md` but the body is written by humans; (b) **whiteboarding-authored session briefs** — the `whiteboarding` skill (driven by the `brainstormer` agent) produces a brainstorming session that arrives via `docs/inbox/` and is filed by `process-inbox` → `propose-brief`. This machine-authored exploration is the sanctioned exception to the human-only rule; an architect still turns the brief into the ADR.
- `docs/promptbooks/active/*` body content — humans may co-author the `goal`, `strategy`, and `prompts` list. Claude owns the run-state fields (status, current_run, current_prompt), the run snapshots under `runs/`, and the archive transition.
  - **New-format note:** promptbook and run files are **structured YAML documents** (one document per book/run), not Markdown frontmatter + body. Humans co-authoring `goal` / `strategy` / `prompts` edit **YAML literal block-scalar (`|`) fields** rather than free Markdown sections — this is more indentation-sensitive, and a YAML syntax error breaks the **whole book** (not just one section). See §3 for layout and the schema pointers; §11.B for the run per-prompt shape. Legacy `.md` books and runs remain readable as history. Live Markdown execution and current-version conversion refuse; the upgrade guide describes the limited tagged recovery route.

- `docs/objectives.md` — the mission and goals (§5.B). Humans own the Mission, Goals, and Shifts content. Claude seeds the placeholder at init, proposes edits in chat or via `docs/inbox/`, appends a Shifts row only on instruction, and never rewrites the mission or a goal on its own.

Everything else — `code/`, `research/`, `adrs/` (after Proposed is reviewed), `journal/`, indexes, `log/`, `manifest.yml` — Claude writes.

